I have enough context now. Let me also check how nef.rs functions are called from elsewhere and if there's a non-linux stub pattern already established. Also need to check if there are other Windows compile issues beyond the two errors the user reported.

Key findings for the plan:

1. **E0433 in nef.rs**: `RemoteNefReader`, `remote_nef_embedded_jpeg`, `remote_nef_orientation` are unconditional (no cfg gate). They call `crate::network_shares::stat/trace` which only exists on Linux. These functions are only called from Linux-gated paths in viewer.rs (lines 49, 203, 443 all have cfg linux before them). Fix: gate the entire remote NEF block (struct + impls + functions) with `#[cfg(target_os="linux")]`. On non-Linux, the functions aren't defined and aren't called (call sites are cfg-gated). But wait - need to verify no other callers of remote_nef_* without cfg.

From grep: remote_nef_embedded_jpeg only called from viewer.rs:49 and :203 (both cfg linux). remote_nef_orientation only from viewer.rs:443 (cfg linux). Good.

Also `crate::source::read_range` in nef.rs - that exists cross-platform (it's in source.rs without cfg). OK.

2. **E0308 in viewer.rs decode_for_viewer_with_cancel**: The structure is:

```rust
let (image, target_width, target_height) = if is_raw(reference) {
    #[cfg(target_os="linux")]
    if crate::network_shares::private(reference){ ... return-ish expression ... } else {
        // local raw path producing (DynamicImage, u32, u32)
    }
} else if is_jpeg...
```

On Windows: the cfg attribute strips only the `if private(...)` part... Actually wait, the cfg is on the `if` expression that's the entire body of the `is_raw` branch. Looking at line 203-261:

```
if is_raw(reference) {
    #[cfg(target_os="linux")]
    if crate::network_shares::private(reference){
 ...produce tuple...
    } else {
      ...local path producing tuple...
    }
} else if is_jpeg...
```

The cfg is attached to the inner `if` expression statement... Actually in Rust, `#[cfg]` on an expression that is the sole content of a block: on non-Linux, the entire if-else expression is removed, leaving an empty block `()` as the value of the is_raw branch. That's the E0308.

Fix: restructure so the network branch is cfg-gated as an early path that still yields the same type, OR restructure the outer if so that on non-linux the is_raw branch is just the local path (unconditional local code).

Best approach matching codebase style (like recovery.rs, cache.rs use cfg blocks):

Option A - restructure outer:
```rust
let (image, target_width, target_height) = if is_raw(reference) {
    #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
    if crate::network_shares::private(reference) {
        // remote branch returning tuple
        ...
    } else {
        // local raw branch
        ...
    }
    #[cfg(not(target_os = "linux"))]
    {
        // local raw branch
        ...
    }
}
```
This duplicates the local raw branch - bad.

Option B - extract local raw decode to helper function `fn decode_local_raw_for_viewer(...) -> Result<(DynamicImage, u32, u32)>` and:

```rust
if is_raw(reference) {
    #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
    if crate::network_shares::private(reference) {
        // remote branch
    } else {
        decode_local_raw_for_viewer(...)?
    }
    #[cfg(not(target_os = "linux"))]
    decode_local_raw_for_viewer(...)?
}
```
Wait - on linux with cfg on if, the else is part of same if. On not-linux, the block expression. Both yield tuple. This works!

Actually careful with cfg on if-else: both branches of the linux if are kept. On not-linux only the second block. Types must match - yes both produce (DynamicImage, u32, u32).

But there's a subtlety: can you use `#[cfg]` on an if expression used as a block value? Yes:

```rust
let x = if cond {
    #[cfg(linux)]
    if a { ... } else { ... }
    #[cfg(not(linux))]
    { ... }
} else { ... };
```

Hmm, actually when you have two cfg'd expressions in sequence inside a block, the block evaluates... only one survives at a time, so it's the tail expression. Good.

Option C - early-return style used in decode_raw_thumbnail_inner: the remote path already does `return`. For the viewer function, the value is bound in a let, so early return from the whole function doesn't work for partial; but we could early-return the entire function result... The remote branch doesn't complete the whole function - it only sets the image tuple, then continues with orientation resize. So can't early-return whole function.

Option D - invert: extract remote network branch into a helper function gated #[cfg(linux)], and use:

```rust
if is_raw(reference) {
    #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
    if crate::network_shares::private(reference) {
        remote_raw_for_viewer(...)?
    } else {
        local_raw_for_viewer(...)?
    }
    #[cfg(not(target_os = "linux"))]
    local_raw_for_viewer(...)?
}
```

Or simpler - move the cfg off the if and onto a helper:

Actually the cleanest minimal fix without huge refactor:

```rust
let (image, target_width, target_height) = if is_raw(reference) {
    #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
    if crate::network_shares::private(reference) {
        ... tuple ...
    } else {
        ... existing local code (lines 204-261) ...
    }
    #[cfg(not(target_os = "linux"))]
    {
        ... duplicate local code ...
    }
}
```

Duplication is bad. Better: extract lines 204-261 into `fn decode_local_raw_viewer(...) -> Result<(DynamicImage, u32, u32)>` - needs cancelled closure, orientation, viewport dims.

Then:
```rust
if is_raw(reference) {
    #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
    if crate::network_shares::private(reference) {
        // remote branch body -> tuple
    } else {
        decode_local_raw_viewer(...)?
    }
    #[cfg(not(target_os = "linux"))]
    decode_local_raw_viewer(...)?
}
```

Also decode_raw_thumbnail_inner (line 47-49): the cfg is on the whole `if private` with return inside - on non-linux, the if is stripped, leaving empty statement, then continues to local path. That works fine (statement position, not value position). No error reported there. Good.

exif_orientation line 441-444: cfg on if with early return - statement position, fine.

3. **nef.rs**: Gate RemoteNefReader and remote_* functions with `#[cfg(target_os="linux")]`. The const REMOTE_NEF_METADATA_READ_AHEAD too. On Windows these are never called. Also need `#[cfg(not(target_os="linux"))]` stubs? No - no callers outside linux cfg.

But wait - dead_code warnings? On Linux all used. On Windows unused items gated out. Perfect.

4. **Other unguarded call sites?** From the audit, all other call sites already have `#[cfg(target_os = "linux")]`:
- source.rs: yes
- scanner.rs: yes  
- cache.rs remote_nef: yes
- recovery.rs: yes
- batch.rs: yes
- photos.rs is_remote_path: yes (plus not-linux stub)
- photo_actions.rs: yes
- build.rs watch: yes
- build.rs network_picker: yes (line 3221 cfg wraps the whole add_network_share_slot.replace)
- availability.rs probe: yes
- viewer.rs: lines 49, 203, 442 - yes but203 has type mismatch issuenetwork_picker only called from build.rs:3242 which is inside `#[cfg(target_os="linux")]` block at 3221. And module itself is cfg linux. Good.

5. **Are there other Windows compile errors?** User only reported these two. After fixing, we need cargo check. On Linux machine we can only `cargo check` for Linux (won't catch Windows issues). Can we `cargo check --target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`? Probably not installed. Could try `--target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` if mingw toolchain exists. Plan should mention: attempt cross-check if toolchain available; at minimum verify Linux cargo check still passes; recommend user re-run cargo build on Windows.

Also check: does the codebase have other cfg(linux)-dependent items that break Windows? e.g. smbclient in Cargo.toml features, platform module, etc. Let me check platform.rs and Cargo.toml briefly for windows support readiness - but user said the errors are just these two. Focus the plan on those; cargo check may reveal more.

Also nef.rs uses `crate::network_shares::stat` - on Windows after gating whole remote block, fine.

What about `source::read_range` on Windows - exists, uses std::fs - works only for local paths; if someone passes smb:// it would fail open - but private() gates prevent that on Windows (private always... wait, private is linux-only, so on Windows network_shares doesn't exist and smb:// URIs go through GIO. That's existing behavior.)

6. **Plan structure**:

### Fix 1: viewer.rs E0308
Extract local RAW viewer branch into helper, use dual-cfg structure so both platforms produce tuple.

### Fix 2: nef.rs E0433  
cfg-gate RemoteNefReader + remote_nef_embedded_jpeg + remote_nef_orientation (+ const).

### Fix 3: Verify remaining sites (already audited - all gated)

### Verification
- cargo fmt
- cargo check (linux)
- cargo check --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/msvc if available, else instruct user
- Optionally cargo test for affected modules

Also check decode_raw_thumbnail_inner - no error on Windows (cfg strips if in statement position). But wait - is the `#[cfg]` on the `if` statement with `return` - after stripping, does `reference` become unused? No, reference used later. Fine.

One more: line 49 decode_raw_thumbnail_inner - the remote path calls remote_nef_embedded_jpeg which if we cfg-gate nef functions to linux only, the call at line 49 is also inside cfg linux. Good.

Should we also add not-linux stub for remote functions for tests? Tests at bottom use decode_raw_thumbnail with local paths only. The cancelled_remote_jpeg test uses smb:// URL with cancel before network - works cross-platform since it never reaches network code (read path would use GIO). Fine.

I'll also double-check: is there anything in `thumbnail/mod.rs` or batch that references remote_nef unguarded? cache.rs remote_nef is gated.

I think I have a complete plan. Present it to the user for approval since we're in plan mode.

Also mention: no hacks - the non-linux branch runs the full local RAW decode path (same as else branch today), which is correct because on Windows there are no private smb/nfs shares; local materialize works.

Let me also verify the exact structure of lines 201-262 once more for the plan's "before/after" sketch.

```rust
let (image, target_width, target_height) = if is_raw(reference) {
    #[cfg(target_os="linux")]
    if crate::network_shares::private(reference){
 ... remote produce tuple
    } else {
      ... local path lines 204-261 producing tuple
    }
} else if is_jpeg(reference) {
```

On Windows: cfg removes if-else expression, body is empty → `()` vs expected tuple. Confirmed.

Fix without helper - another pattern used in this codebase for value-producing cfg:

```rust
if is_raw(reference) {
    #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
    if crate::network_shares::private(reference) {
        ...remote...
    } else {
        local_raw(...)?
    }
    #[cfg(not(target_os = "linux"))]
    local_raw(...)?
}
```

I'll recommend extracting `decode_local_raw_for_viewer` helper to avoid duplication.

Actually wait - could also restructure as:

```rust
if is_raw(reference) {
    #[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
    if crate::network_shares::private(reference) {
        remote_tuple
    } else {
        local_tuple
    }
    #[cfg(not(target_os = "linux"))]
    {
        local_tuple  // full code
    }
}
```

Duplication of ~55 lines - bad. Helper is better.

Alternatively invert the linux check:

```rust
#[cfg(target_os = "linux")]
let network_raw = crate::network_shares::private(reference);
#[cfg(not(target_os = "linux"))]
let network_raw = false;

if is_raw(reference) {
